window.docContents[187].resolve({"/ProofObjects/#Logical-Foundations--ProofObjects___-The-Curry-Howard-Correspondence--Equality":{"contents":"\n\nnamespace EqualityPlayground\n\n\nEven Lean's equality relation is not built in.  We can define\nit ourselves:inductive Eq {α : Type} : α → α → Prop where\n  | refl : ∀ (x : α), Eq x x\n\nscoped infix:50 \" =' \" => Eq\nThe Eq definition relates pairs of elements of type α. The only way to\nprove Eq a b for a pair of elements a and b is via the Eq.refl constructor,\nwhich by requiring Eq x x for all x forces a and b to be the same.This means we can use Eq.refl to construct evidence that, for example, 2 = 2.\nCan we also use it to construct evidence that 1 + 1 = 2?\nPerhaps surprisingly: Yes, we can!The reason is that Lean treats as \"the same\" any two terms that are\nconvertible according to a simple set of computation rules.\nThese rules, which are similar to those used by #eval, include\nevaluation of function application, inlining of definitions, and\nsimplification of match expressions.theorem four : 2 + 2 =' 1 + 3 := by\n  apply Eq.refl\nThe rfl tactic that we have used to prove\nequalities of the form x = y up to now is\nessentially just shorthand for apply Eq.refl.In tactic-based proofs of equality, the conversion rules operate\ninvisibly: whenever Lean checks two terms for definitional equality\n— as rfl does when closing a goal x = y, or as exact\nand apply do when unifying a term against an expected type — it\nreduces both sides by these computation rules before comparing them.\nYou can see what's going on by enabling the Lean option trace.Meta.isDefEq:set_option trace.Meta.isDefEq true in\nexample : 1 + 1 = 2 := rfl\nIn explicit proofs of equality, the conversion rules still operate\ninvisibly, but their result is made manifest as an argument to\nEq.refl in the proof term.theorem four' : 2 + 2 =' 1 + 3 :=\n  Eq.refl 4\n\ntheorem singleton : ∀ (α : Type) (x : α),\n    [] ++ [x] =' x :: [] :=\n  fun (α : Type) (x : α) => Eq.refl [x]\nWe can also pattern-match on an equality proof:theorem eq_add : ∀ (n1 n2 : Nat),\n    n1 =' n2 → (n1 + 1) =' (n2 + 1) :=\n  fun n1 n2 heq =>\n    match heq with\n    | Eq.refl _ => Eq.refl (n1 + 1)\nBy pattern-matching against n1 =' n2, we replace n2 with n1\nin the type we have to produce, so\ninstead of (n1 + 1) =' (n2 + 1), we now have to produce something\nof type (n1 + 1) =' (n1 + 1), which we establish by Eq.refl (n1 + 1).A tactic-based proof runs into some difficulties if we try to use\nour usual repertoire of tactics, such as rw and\nrfl. Those work with relations that Lean knows about,\nsuch as =, but not with our ='. We could teach Lean about ='\n(by registering a reflexivity lemma for rfl, or by working\nin a quotient type where =' becomes =, for rw),\nbut a simpler way is to use cases and apply instead.example (n1 n2 : Nat) (heq : n1 =' n2) :\n    (n1 + 1) =' (n2 + 1) := by\n  rw [heq] -- doesn't work for _our_ =' relation\nexample (n1 : Nat) : (n1 + 1) =' (n1 + 1) := by\n  rfl -- doesn't work for _our_ =' relation\ntheorem eq_add' (n1 n2 : Nat) (heq : n1 =' n2) :\n    (n1 + 1) =' (n2 + 1) := by\n  cases heq -- n2 replaced by n1 in the goal!\n  apply Eq.refl\nConstruct the proof object for the following theorem. Use pattern\nmatching on the equality hypotheses.theorem eq_cons : ∀ (α : Type) (h1 h2 : α) (t1 t2 : List α),\n    h1 =' h2 → t1 =' t2 → h1 :: t1 =' h2 :: t2 :=\n  solution!(fun _ h1 _ t1 _ heq teq =>\n    match heq, teq with\n    | Eq.refl _, Eq.refl _ => Eq.refl (h1 :: t1))\n(PR) The leibniz_equality exercise may confuse students\nbecause of the reuse of = for local equality. In the solution, the\ninversion cannot be replaced with a rewrite, as it can outside this\nmodule, because rewrite does not understand local equality. A quick\nfix is to use notation such as \"x == y\" locally.  Then it is clear\nthat the inversion replaces local equality with library\nequality. But to really clarify, perhaps the exercise should be\nmoved to IndPrinciples.v, where the text can show the actual\ndefinition of library equality (after the discussion of the simpler\nprinciple for le to motivate the use of a parameter) and the\nresulting eq_ind, and the exercise can ask for an explicit proof\nterm instead of a tactics-based proof.\n\n(BCP) At least one reader did get confused by the rewrite failure.\nAnd the harder exercise is also confusing for the same reason (have\nto use apply instead of reflexivity).  Would be good to do\nsomething about this.  I agree with moving it elsewhere, I think.\n\n(PR) I have put the == quick fix in.\n\nMRC 3/22: I have added [eq_add'] above to explain why [rewrite] and\n[reflexivity] fail, and to show that [destruct] and [apply] will\nwork.  I've also added a second exercise to give the proof object,\nwhich does not actually require [eq_ind]. I think the exercise\nstands on its own here, now.\nThe inductive definition of equality implies Leibniz equality:\nwhat we mean when we say \"x and y are equal\" is that every\nproperty P that is true of x is also true of y. Prove\nthat.theorem equality__leibniz_equality : ∀ (α : Type) (x y : α),\n    x =' y → ∀ (P : α → Prop), P x → P y := by\n  solution!\n    intro α x y heq P hpx\n    cases heq\n    exact hpx\nConstruct the proof object for the previous exercise.  All it\nrequires is anonymous functions and pattern-matching; the large\nproof term constructed by tactics in the previous exercise is\nneedessly complicated. Hint: pattern-match as soon as possible.theorem equality__leibniz_equality_term :\n    ∀ (α : Type) (x y : α),\n    x =' y → ∀ (P : α → Prop), P x → P y :=\n  solution!(fun _ _ _ heq =>\n    match heq with\n    | Eq.refl _ => fun _ hp => hp)\nShow that, in fact, the inductive definition of equality is\nequivalent to Leibniz equality.  Hint: the proof is quite short.\nYou can write it by inventing a clever property P to\ninstantiate the antecedent, or you can let Lean figure it out...theorem leibniz_equality__equality : ∀ (α : Type) (x y : α),\n    (∀ (P : α → Prop), P x → P y) → x =' y := by\n  solution!\n    intro α x y h\n    apply h (fun z => x =' z) -- `apply h` also works; Lean will provide `P`.\n    apply Eq.refl\n\n\nWhich of the following is a correct proof object for the proposition∃ x, x + 3 =' 4\n?(A) Eq.refl 4(B) @Exists.intro Nat (z + 3 =' 4) 1 (Eq.refl 4)(C) @Exists.intro Nat (fun z => z + 3 =' 4) 1 (Eq.refl 4)(D) @Exists.intro Nat (fun z => z + 3 =' 4) 1 (Eq.refl 1)(E) none of the abovetheorem quiz1 : ∃ x, x + 3 =' 4 :=\n  Eq.refl 4\ntheorem quiz2 : ∃ x, x + 3 =' 4 :=\n  @Exists.intro Nat (z + 3 =' 4) 1 (Eq.refl 4)\ntheorem quiz3 : ∃ x, x + 3 =' 4 :=\n  @Exists.intro Nat (fun z => z + 3 =' 4) 1 (Eq.refl 4)\ntheorem quiz4 : ∃ x, x + 3 =' 4 :=\n  @Exists.intro Nat (fun z => z + 3 =' 4) 1 (Eq.refl 1)\n\n\nend EqualityPlayground\n\n\n-- ## Inversion, Again-- :::suppressPreviousHeaderWhenTerse\n-- :::HIDE: PR: This section may be repetitive given the increased\nattention paid to inversion, and it may be ineffective since\nthis chapter is no longer on the recommended path. We need to\nthink about whether this kind of reinforcement of the tactic is\nneeded, and if so, where to place it for best impact.BCP 11/18: I agree — this material is a bit redundant.  On the\nother hand, the examples at the end may be helpful, so maybe it's\nfine to leave it.\n\n","context":"Logical Foundations\u0009ProofObjects: The Curry-Howard Correspondence","header":"10.7. Equality","id":"/ProofObjects/#Logical-Foundations--ProofObjects___-The-Curry-Howard-Correspondence--Equality"},"/Tactics/#Logical-Foundations--Tactics___-More-Basic-Tactics--Rewriting-with-Conditional-Statements":{"contents":"Rewriting with a conditional theorem is similar in spirit to backward\nreasoning with apply, introduced earlier in this chapter: both let\na theorem's premises become new subgoals. Here, though, it is rw\ndoing the work, using the theorem's conclusion to transform the goal rather\nthan closing it outright.\n\nSuppose that we know two numbers have the same double, and we want to\nuse this fact to rewrite one of them into the other. Recall the theorem\ndouble_injective from the previous section:#check double_injective\ndouble_injective (n m : Nat) (h : n.double = m.double) : n = m\nFor example, we can prove:\n\nexample (n m p q : Nat)\n    (h : n.double = m.double)\n    (hm : m + p = q) :\n    n + p = q := by\n  rw [double_injective n m]\n  · assumption\n  · assumption\n\n\nThe use of rw here is a little different from the examples we have seen so far.\nThe theorem double_injective says n = m\nprovided that n.double = m.double, not just n = m.\nWhen we write rw [double_injective n m], Lean uses the conclusion n = m to rewrite\nthe goal, and then asks us to prove the hypothesis needed by double_injective.\nThus we get two goals: the updated main goal, m + p = q, and the\ncondition from double_injective, n.double = m.double.\nThese goals follow by assumption from hm and h, respectively.\n\nIf we rewrite with a conditional statement of the form\nP → a = b, then Lean tries to rewrite with a = b, and then\nasks us to prove P in a new subgoal.  If the statement has more\nthan one assumption, then we get one subgoal for each assumption.\n\n","context":"Logical Foundations\u0009Tactics: More Basic Tactics","header":"7.6. Rewriting with Conditional Statements","id":"/Tactics/#Logical-Foundations--Tactics___-More-Basic-Tactics--Rewriting-with-Conditional-Statements"}});